During the [[@world-war-one|First World War]], Britain printed a poster of Lord Kitchener pointing at the viewer, telling men their country needed them. In 1917, the American artist James Montgomery Flagg drew Uncle Sam pointing the same way, with the words "I want YOU for U.S. Army". Millions of copies were printed.

Both sides made posters showing the enemy as monsters and their own soldiers as heroes. Simple images, bold words and strong feelings made the messages hard to forget.

{{story:world-war-one}}

For decades, films relied on clever handmade work. Small models of ships, buildings and spacecraft were filmed close up so they looked huge. Artists painted scenery on glass, called matte paintings, and filmed through the glass so real actors appeared inside painted cities.

In *King Kong* (1933), the giant ape was a model less than half a metre tall, moved a tiny bit between each frame. This is the same stop-motion idea used in [[@animation-studios|animation]]. Monster suits, rubber masks and puppets filled films for years after.

